From 6b7eec434a7aa41d29965aefdfe708f859913875 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 07:57:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] IntegrationRuleOrder: order of the integration rules chosen
 by the user

The behaviour integrators choose their integration rules by the order
`2 * t.OrderGrad(&e)`: 27 points on the hexahedra of order 1, while 8
points integrate exactly the elastic stiffness of affine hexahedra of
order 1. The new optional parameter `IntegrationRuleOrder` of the finite
element discretization gives the order of the integration rules of all
the behaviour integrators (`applyIntegrationRuleOrder`): with the order
3, the hexahedra of order 1 have 8 points, which divides by about 3.4 the
memory and the work at the integration points. The order is the same for
all the geometries and orders of the elements (documented: the order 3
under-integrates the hexahedra of order 2, the orders 0 and 1 give one
point on the hexahedra). A negative order, and a rule having a weight
which is not positive (rules of MFEM for some simplices), are refused.

The behaviour integrators build their quadrature spaces with this rule,
and their `getIntegrationRule` method returns the rule of their
quadrature space: the integrations, the quadrature spaces and the
computations by kernels use the same rule.

New test IntegrationRuleOrderTest (on the host and on the debug device of
MFEM): numbers of points (27 by default, 8 with the order 3), same
solution on affine hexahedra point by point and by kernels, rule of the
behaviour integrators checked before the resolution, refusal of a
negative order. Documentation: developer guide, glossary, change log.
